.TH CHTTP_TLS_DEFAULT 3 "2026-09-23" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
CHTTP_TLS_DEFAULT \- the initializer for the default TLS configuration
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttp.h>

CHTTP_TLS_DEFAULT
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro expands to an initializer for a chttp_tls_config_t with:
  insecure_skip_verify         = false (verify the certificate chain)
  insecure_skip_hostname_check = false (match the hostname)
  ca_bundle_path               = NULL  (use the system CA bundle)
  cert_path                    = NULL  (no client certificate)
  key_path                     = NULL  (no client key)
  client_cert_optional         = false (a server with ca_bundle_path
                                        requires a client certificate)
.fi
.PP
Every field of the default is zero, so this macro and a zero-initialized
struct name the same settings. A chttp_tls_config_t that a caller builds
with = {0}, or with a designated initializer that names only some of the
fields, therefore verifies the chain of the server and matches its
hostname on a client. On a server, such a struct with a ca_bundle_path
requires every client to present a certificate that verifies against
it. A caller cannot reach a weaker setting by writing less than
they meant to. Weakening a check takes an explicit assignment, and the
name of the field says what that assignment costs.
.PP
insecure_skip_verify accepts any certificate from any peer. An attacker
who can intercept the connection can then read and rewrite every byte of
it. It also makes insecure_skip_hostname_check irrelevant, because a
hostname matched against a certificate that nothing validated proves
nothing. There is deliberately no way to ask for the hostname check
without the chain check.
.PP
insecure_skip_hostname_check is the narrower relaxation. It keeps the
full chain verification and drops only the match of the hostname against
the subject of the certificate. Use it to reach a host by an address or
an internal name that its certificate does not carry, while still
requiring that the certificate chains to a CA that you pinned with
ca_bundle_path.
.PP
client_cert_optional is a setting of the server alone, and a client
ignores it. It turns the requirement of a client certificate into a
request: the server still refuses a certificate that does not verify,
and also serves a client that presents none.
chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_verified(3) then tells a handler which kind of
client it serves.
.PP
cert_path, key_path and ca_bundle_path must each name a regular file of
at most 16 MB.  A path that names a directory, a FIFO or a device is
refused at once as a load failure, before a byte of it is read.
.PP
A ca_bundle_path holds one or more CA certificates in PEM form, and may
hold certificate revocation lists (CRLs) beside them.  A file of CRLs
alone names no CA and is refused.  When the bundle holds a CRL,
revocation is checked for the whole chain that the peer presents, as
the ssl_crl directive of nginx does: every certificate of that chain
below its trust anchor needs a CRL of its issuer in the bundle.  The
handshake then fails for a revoked certificate, for a certificate whose
issuer has no CRL in the bundle, and for a CRL past its next update
time, because a chain that cannot be checked is not accepted.  An
operator who enables revocation for a chain of a root and an
intermediate CA therefore puts the CRL of the root, which covers the
intermediate, and the CRL of the intermediate, which covers the leaf,
into the bundle, and renews both before they expire.  A bundle with no
CRL checks no revocation.  The library reads the bundle when it applies
the configuration, so a renewed CRL takes effect at the next
chttpsvr_start(3) of a server, or the next chttpclient_set_tls(3) of a
client.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
/* The defaults: verify the chain and the hostname. */
chttp_tls_config_t tls = CHTTP_TLS_DEFAULT;
chttpclient_set_tls(cli, &tls);

/* The same defaults, written as a zero-initialized struct. */
chttp_tls_config_t same = {0};
chttpclient_set_tls(cli, &same);

/* Pin a private CA and skip only the hostname match. */
chttp_tls_config_t pinned = {
    .ca_bundle_path = "/etc/myapp/ca-chain.pem",
    .insecure_skip_hostname_check = true,
};
chttpclient_set_tls(cli, &pinned);

/* Accept any certificate. Development and throwaway self-signed
   servers only; this gives an interceptor full control of the
   connection. */
chttp_tls_config_t insecure = {.insecure_skip_verify = true};
chttpclient_set_tls(cli, &insecure);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpclient_set_tls (3),
.BR chttpsvr_start (3),
.BR chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_verified (3)
